1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,648
Narrator: Scenario 35. Cloud platforms, containers, and automation.

2
00:00:07,248 --> 00:00:18,648
A: We need to drain node seven for operating system maintenance. Have we checked workload disruption budgets, replacement capacity, and any storage tied to this node?

3
00:00:19,248 --> 00:00:31,536
B: One application has three replicas, but only two are healthy. Its disruption budget requires two available replicas, so evicting the local healthy pod is blocked.

4
00:00:32,136 --> 00:00:42,696
A: Let us restore redundancy before proceeding with maintenance. Do we know why the third replica is unhealthy, and is the application owner already involved?

5
00:00:43,296 --> 00:00:54,576
B: The third replica cannot connect to its required queue because of a configuration mistake. The application owner is preparing a correction through the change process.

6
00:00:55,176 --> 00:01:05,952
A: Please keep the drain paused. We should not override the disruption budget simply to meet the maintenance schedule, especially while the service has reduced redundancy.

7
00:01:06,552 --> 00:01:18,984
B: Agreed. The remaining nodes have capacity for the evicted workloads. I also confirmed that this application uses shared storage and has no node-local persistent data.

8
00:01:19,584 --> 00:01:29,904
A: After the correction, wait until all three replicas are ready and stable. Can the application owner run a representative request before we resume?

9
00:01:30,504 --> 00:01:43,008
B: Yes. They will verify queue processing and a completed customer operation. We will retain the evidence in the maintenance ticket rather than rely only on pod status.

10
00:01:43,608 --> 00:01:54,624
A: Once that passes, cordon the node and drain through normal eviction handling. Watch replacement readiness and stop if another replica becomes unhealthy during the move.

11
00:01:55,224 --> 00:02:06,408
B: I will perform the drain and maintenance one node at a time. After reboot, I will validate networking, storage access, and node health before uncordoning.

12
00:02:07,008 --> 00:02:18,936
A: Let us use 19:00 UTC as the readiness checkpoint. If redundancy is not restored by then, we will reschedule this node and notify the maintenance owner.

13
00:02:19,536 --> 00:02:30,384
B: I will update the ticket at that checkpoint. We will close only after workload health remains stable and the application owner confirms the post-maintenance checks.
